00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:45.160
So onto today's topic:

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:54.540
Imagine that there was a mysterious language in Europe 
that was surrounded on all sides by languages 
that it had absolutely no connection with.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:59.140
Well there actually is a language like that. 
The language is the Basque language.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:06.260
Basque is a language isolate, meaning that 
it has no known connection to any other language.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:09.920
It's located in Europe, 
but it is not an Indo-European language.

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:15.400
It forms its own language family and it is quite distinct, 
very different, from Indo-European languages

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:22.760
It is spoken by the Basque people in the Basque country, a region that spans the Spain-France border
in the western-most Pyrenees mountains.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:29.220
It is not spoken by all Basques, but by 
around 27% of them in the Basque country overall.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:38.320
The number of native speakers is 714,135 
out of a total population of 2,648,998,

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:44.100
and that includes about 663,000 on the Spanish side, 
and 51,100 on the French side.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:49.940
It is an official language at the regional level in Spain, 
in the Basque Autonomous Community, and in Navarre.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:53.300
In France, it holds no official status.

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:59.180
The origins of the Basque language are shrouded in mystery. As I said before, it is a language isolate.

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:05.340
It is thought to be the last remaining language 
that existed in Europe before the arrival 
of the Indo-European languages.

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:08.980
There are some other pre-Indo-European
languages in the Caucasus Region, 
but Basque is the only one in Western Europe.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:15.880
There is conclusive evidence that Basque is 
a descendant of the Aquitanian language, which 
is an ancient language spoken in the Pyrenees region.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:22.240
Some similarities are known between Aquitanian 
and the ancient Iberian language, 
so some people think that they might be related.

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:28.000
But those similarities might just be due to geographic proximity and mutual influence. We don't know.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:33.320
Some linguists suggest that Aquitanian was part of
a wider language family called the Vasconic languages,

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:37.000
which covered most of Europe before the arrival 
of the Indo-European languages.

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:38.700
But, again, we don't know.

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:44.160
Other people draw a connection between Basque 
and other non-Indo-European languages 
like the Caucasian languages.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:44.940
But again...

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:46.500
(Let me guess, we don't know?)

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:48.000
that's right, we don't know.

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:54.600
One thing we do know is that the Basque people, 
who descend mostly from farmers who arrived 
in the area around 6,000 years ago,

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:57.960
were isolated from the outside world for thousands of years.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:02.000
That's probably partly because of 
the Basque country's forested mountain terrain,

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:05.500
and its lack of tempting resources
that prevented invasion.

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:11.320
This isolation is probably what allowed their language to 
survive and develop into the Basque language of today.

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:13.740
Of course, there were Latin borrowings into the language,

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:17.020
and there have been other Romance language borrowings throughout the centuries,

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:21.220
but nothing like the total disappearance 
of other pre-Latin languages in that area.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:26.000
Basque was a unified language until the Middle Ages, 
when it began to diverge into dialects,

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:29.680
because of administrative and political divisions within the Basque country.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:33.000
Despite being ruled by outside regimes 
throughout the centuries,

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:39.420
the Basque country still remained isolated 
and relatively uninfluenced by the outside world, 
and that includes the language.

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:45.760
But when Francisco Franco became ruler of Spain 
in 1939, the use of Basque was heavily suppressed,

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:49.500
because Franco wanted to assimilate
all of Spain into castilian culture.

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:55.700
It was forbidden to speak Basque in schools 
and in public. It was banned from media
and removed from public services.

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:59.620
This led to a big reduction in the number of people 
who could speak the Basque language,

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:04.800
and that's part of the reason that only 27% of people 
in the Basque country speak the language today.

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:09.700
In the 1960s, that suppression was eased somewhat, 
and Basque language schools became permitted,

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:14.000
and the language began being used 
in publications and in education again.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:18.200
This led to the creation of a standardised language called Euskara Batua.

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:22.140
It was developed by the Basque Language Academy, 
or the "Euskaltzaindia",

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:26.220
and it was intended to be comprehensible 
to the speakers of the various dialects of Basque.

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:32.420
There are 5 main Basque dialects: Bizkaian 
or Western Basque, Gipuzkoan or Central Basque,

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:37.000
Upper Navarese, Navarro-Lapurdian, 
and Souletin in France.

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:40.840
These dialects correspond with 
the historic provinces of the Basque country,

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:44.500
but they don't completely correspond
 with the modern provinces of today.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:49.600
The level of intelligibility depends on the distance between those two dialects on the dialect continuum,

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:53.000
with the most distant dialects 
having trouble understanding each other.

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:57.700
But that's where the standard language, 
Euskara Batua, comes in. 
So, what is the Basque language like?

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:02.180
Well, its vocabulary has been influenced by the surrounding Romance languages to some extent.

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:06.100
But when you look at its structure,
you'll see that it is unlike any Romance language,

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:08.660
or like any Indo-European language for that matter.

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:15.000
Basque has grammatical cases, 12 cases to be exact, but that is not really unusual in Indo-European languages,

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:17.280
but it has something called the "ergotive case".

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:22.680
That means that there's a special form of the noun 
when it's the subject of a sentence and takes a transitive verb.

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:27.840
That means it has a direct object. This ergotive case 
is marked by a "k" at the end of the noun.

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:31.700
Now, along with the ergotive case,
there's also something called the "absolutive case".

00:05:31.820 --> 00:05:36.000
This is for subjects of intransitive verbs, 
meaning that it has no direct object,

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:38.540
and in this case there is no ending 
on the end of the noun.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:40.680
Well, let's take a look at a couple of sentences.

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:42.800
[Basque-speaking voice: umea kalean erori da]

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:49.300
That means, "The child fell in the street", 
but if we look at it word by word, 
you can see the interesting structure of Basque,

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:53.840
Word-by-word it's "child-the", "street-the-in", "fall", "is".

00:05:54.520 --> 00:06:00.000
If we look at the first word there: "ume" is child, 
but then the definite article is the "a" at the end.

00:06:00.140 --> 00:06:07.120
Then for the next word, "kalean", "kale" is street, 
and then the definite article is "a", 
and then "in" is the "n" at the end of the word.

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:14.000
If we look at the next word, "erori",
that's a verb meaning "fall", but it's in the Perfect Aspect, 
that means it shows the completed action.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:20.660
And then the auxiliary verb comes after the main verb, 
and this one means "is". 
It's the present-tense form of "to be".

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:21.840
And another sentence,

00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:24.860
[Basque-speaking voice: 
gizonak umeari liburua eman dio]

00:06:24.980 --> 00:06:27.480
That means "The man has given the book to the child".

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:31.700
So, word-by-word, "man-the", in the ergotive case.

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:33.920
"child-the", dative case,

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:36.040
"book-the", "given", "has"

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:43.220
The first word is "gizonak", "gizon" is "man", then 
the definite article is "a", and the the ergotive case is "k".

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:51.780
"umeari", that's "child". Again, "child" is "ume", 
and then this time it has the definite article, "a", 
and then it has the dative case marker, "ri", at the end

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:55.480
The dative case basically shows 
who or what is being affected by the action.

00:06:55.640 --> 00:07:02.400
The next word, "liburua", "liburu" is "book", 
and "a" is the definitive article, and this one looks 
like a loan word from a romance language,

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:07.220
and the next word, "eman" is a verb,
 and it's in the Perfect Aspect, 
showing that the action has been completed.

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:11.100
And then the auxiliary verb, dio, 
comes after, and that means "has".

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:12.860
And the next sentence,

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:14.920
[Basque-speaking voice: emakumeak gizona ikusi du]

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:17.120
That means, "The woman has seen the man".

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:22.860
So, word-by-word, "woman-the" ergotive case,
"Man-the", "seen", "has".

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:29.760
So the first word, "emakumeak", "emakume" is "woman", 
"the" is the "a", and the ergotive case is the "k".

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:33.660
The next word, "gizona", that's "the man", 
and the definite article again,

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:39.060
the next word is "ikusi", which means "seen", 
and again, that's a verb with the Perfect aspect 
showing the action's completed.

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:42.920
And then the auxiliary verb comes 
at the end, and it means "has".

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:47.540
As you can see, the Basque language
is very different from any Indo-European language,

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:52.460
very different from any language that I've [ever] studied, 
but it also looks quite logical and systematic.

00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:57.980
It would be a shame to lose a language that's so unique 
and that connects us with the ancient history of Europe.

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:01.500
The number of Basque speakers 
has sharply declined over the last century,

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:06.340
but there are efforts in Spain to revive the language,
and to make it more widespread again,

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:10.820
and hopefully such efforts will continue 
and become more prominent in France as well.

